const schemas = [breadcrumbSchema(breadcrumbs), faqSchema(permitFaqs)];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Saratoga Springs Basement Permit Guide | Utah County Basement Pros"
  description={description}
  path="/saratoga-springs-basement-finishing-permit/"
  schemas={schemas}
>
  <PageIntro
    eyebrow="Permit Guide"
    heading="Saratoga Springs Basement Finishing Permit Guide"
    lede="Saratoga Springs publishes a basement permit page, an online portal, and its own code checklist. This guide puts them in order for a homeowner, with every fact linked to its source: the city, the Utah Code, or the building code Utah has adopted."
    breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  >
    <p class="checked">
      Last checked against city and state sources:
      <time datetime={lastChecked.iso}>{lastChecked.display}</time>
    </p>
  </PageIntro>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container container--narrow prose guide">
      <nav class="jump" aria-label="On this page">
        <ul>
          <li><a href="#plan">The plan</a></li>
          <li><a href="#who">Who needs one</a></li>
          <li><a href="#apply">How to apply</a></li>
          <li><a href="#checklist">Code checklist</a></li>
          <li><a href="#fee">What it costs</a></li>
          <li><a href="#inspections">Inspections</a></li>
          <li><a href="#iadu">Basement apartments</a></li>
          <li><a href="#sources">Sources</a></li>
        </ul>
      </nav>

      <div class="glance">
        <span class="eyebrow">At a glance</span>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Permit:</strong> required. The city's Building
            Department reviews the plan, issues the permit, and inspects
            the work.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Where to apply:</strong> the city's online Public
            Portal. The department is paperless.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>What to send:</strong> a basement plan showing rooms,
            dimensions, doors, windows, lights, outlets, alarms, the
            mechanical room, and the electrical panel.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Fee:</strong> based on the project's valuation, plus
            plan review and a 1% state charge. No flat fee is published.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Inspections:</strong> requested through the Building
            Department's inspection line.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Review time:</strong> not published for basement
            permits.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2 id="plan">Start with the plan</h2>
      <p>
        The drawing Saratoga Springs asks for is a basement plan. The
        city's
        <a href={src.basementPermit} rel="noopener">basement permit
        page</a>
        says it should show:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>the type of each room (bedroom, family room, and so on)</li>
        <li>the dimensions of each room</li>
        <li>the location and size of doors and windows</li>
        <li>the location of lights and outlets</li>
        <li>
          the location of smoke detectors and carbon monoxide detectors
        </li>
        <li>the utility or mechanical room</li>
        <li>the electrical panel</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The same page asks you to read its list of minimum code
        requirements before you draw. The parts that shape a layout are in
        the <a href="#checklist">code checklist</a> below. If the homeowner
        will be doing the work, the city also asks for its Owner Builder
        Certification, found on its
        <a href={src.forms} rel="noopener">Applications and Forms
        page</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="who">Who needs a permit</h2>
      <p>
        Anyone finishing a basement in the city. The
        <a href={src.building} rel="noopener">Building Department</a>
        is responsible for plan reviews, permits, and inspections on
        basement finishes. The requirement itself comes from the code the
        city enforces, the 2021 International Residential Code with Utah's
        amendments
        (<a href={src.utahAdopted} rel="noopener">Utah Code 15A-2-103</a>).
        It requires a permit to alter a building or to install or change
        electrical, gas, mechanical, or plumbing systems
        (<a href={src.ircCh1} rel="noopener">IRC R105.1</a>).
      </p>
      <p>
        Cosmetic work is exempt. Paint, wallpaper, tile, carpet, cabinets,
        countertops, and similar finish work do not need a permit on their
        own (<a href={src.ircCh1} rel="noopener">IRC R105.2</a>).
      </p>

      <h2 id="apply">How to apply</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Go to the Public Portal.</strong> The Building
          Department is paperless. Permit submittals, resubmittals, and
          payments go through the city's
          <a href={src.portal} rel="noopener">Cityworks Public Portal</a>,
          which the basement permit page links as its Basement Permit
          Application.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Complete it in full.</strong> The city asks you to
          finish the portal application and provide all necessary
          documents to avoid delay.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Wait for the approved set.</strong> After the permit is
          issued, the city sends a digital copy of the approved plans and
          permit documents for you to print and keep on site.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Resubmit any changes.</strong> The city's
          <a href={src.buildingPermit} rel="noopener">building permit
          page</a>
          says changes from the approved plans must be resubmitted to the
          Building Department before you make them.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        For application questions, the basement permit page gives
        <a
          href={`mailto:${cityEmail}`}
          data-event="city_contact_click"
          data-event-label="saratoga-building-email"
        >{cityEmail}</a>. The Building Department's phone is
        <a
          href={cityPhone.href}
          data-event="city_contact_click"
          data-event-label="saratoga-building-phone"
        >{cityPhone.display}</a>, and its posted hours are Monday through
        Thursday, 7 AM to 6 PM, and Friday, 8 AM to 5 PM.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Review time:</strong> the city does not publish one for
        basement permits. Its building permit page says plans for
        single-family homes can be processed in approximately 10 working
        days and that more complex plans may take longer. That figure is
        for new homes, not basement finishes.
      </p>

      <h2 id="checklist">The city's code checklist, room by room</h2>
      <p>
        Saratoga Springs publishes its own list of minimum requirements
        for basements on the
        <a href={src.basementPermit} rel="noopener">basement permit
        page</a>, drawn from the 2021 International Residential Code. The
        city notes that the list is not all inclusive. These are the items
        that shape most layouts.
      </p>

      <h3>Bedrooms and windows</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Bedrooms: at least 70 square feet, with no dimension under 7
          feet.
        </li>
        <li>
          Each bedroom window needs a net opening of at least 5.7 square
          feet, at least 24 inches high and 20 inches wide, with the sill
          no more than 44 inches off the floor.
        </li>
        <li>
          Bedroom window wells must extend at least 36 inches out from the
          building. A well deeper than 44 inches needs a permanently
          attached ladder.
        </li>
        <li>
          Windows must provide natural light equal to 8% of the room's
          area, unless specifically approved otherwise.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Ceilings and hallways</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Ceilings at least 7 feet from finished floor to finished
          ceiling.
        </li>
        <li>
          Under beams, girders, ducts, and other obstructions: at least 6
          feet 4 inches.
        </li>
        <li>Hallways at least 36 inches wide, finished.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Bathrooms</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Toilet: 21 inches of clear space in front and 15 inches from its
          center to each side.
        </li>
        <li>
          Shower: 900 square inches of floor with no dimension under 30
          inches, a 22 inch finished opening, a door that swings out, and
          6 feet 8 inches of headroom.
        </li>
        <li>
          Ventilation: a window of at least 3 square feet with 1.5 square
          feet that opens, or an exhaust fan rated at 50 cfm that vents
          directly outside.
        </li>
        <li>GFCI protection for bathroom outlets.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Smoke and carbon monoxide detectors</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          A smoke detector in each bedroom, in the area leading to the
          bedrooms, and at least one on each floor.
        </li>
        <li>Carbon monoxide detectors on each level of the home.</li>
        <li>
          Detectors also go in the existing bedrooms when the remodel
          needs a permit. Where wiring them would mean removing finished
          walls or ceilings, the city allows a battery detector.
        </li>
        <li>
          Wireless interconnection is allowed. Hardwired power is still
          required.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Furnace and water heater</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Not located in a bedroom, bathroom, or similar confined room,
          and not reached through a bedroom or bathroom.
        </li>
        <li>Each one removable without moving the other.</li>
        <li>30 inches of clear working space in front of the furnace.</li>
        <li>Outside combustion air for mechanical equipment.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Electrical</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Outlets no more than 12 feet apart along a wall and within 6
          feet of door openings. Any wall section 2 feet or wider gets
          one.
        </li>
        <li>
          Tamper-resistant outlets, unless more than 5.5 feet above the
          floor.
        </li>
        <li>
          A switched light at hallways, stairways, exits, and in each
          room.
        </li>
        <li>
          Clear working space 36 inches deep and 30 inches wide in front
          of the electrical panel, which can't sit in a clothes closet.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Stairs</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          At least 36 inches wide, finished, with 6 feet 8 inches of
          headroom.
        </li>
        <li>Risers no taller than 8 inches. Treads at least 9 inches.</li>
        <li>
          A handrail on at least one side when there are more than three
          risers, 34 to 38 inches high.
        </li>
        <li>
          Enclosed usable space under the stairs: 1/2 inch drywall on the
          walls and ceiling.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The city's page also covers framing, plumbing, insulation,
        drywall, and wood stoves and fireplaces. Read the full list
        before you finalize a plan.
      </p>

      <h2 id="fee">What it costs</h2>
      <p>
        Saratoga Springs does not publish a flat basement permit fee. Here
        is what its
        <a href={src.feeSchedule} rel="noopener">Consolidated Fee
        Schedule</a>
        does say:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Permit fee.</strong> Set by the fee table in the
          International Residential Code, which the city posts as its
          <a href={src.permitFeeTable} rel="noopener">Building Permit Fee
          Schedule</a>. The fee rises with the project's valuation: $69
          for the first $2,000 plus $11 for each additional $1,000 up to
          $40,000, then $487 for the first $40,000 plus $9 for each
          additional $1,000 up to $100,000.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Valuation.</strong> Set at 75% of the Building Valuation
          Data published by the International Code Council. The schedule
          does not list a per-square-foot figure for finishing a basement.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Plan review.</strong> 65% of the permit fee, or 25% for
          permits that do not require a complete plan review. The schedule
          does not say which one a basement finish falls under.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>State charge.</strong> 1% of the permit fee.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Deposit.</strong> The city's
          <a href={src.buildingPermit} rel="noopener">building permit
          page</a>
          says a non-refundable deposit is due with building permit
          applications, $200 for single family, and is applied toward the
          cost of the permit. The basement permit page does not mention
          the deposit, so check what the portal shows as due.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Reinspection.</strong> $47 per hour, with a one hour
          minimum.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <div class="glance">
        <span class="eyebrow">Worth a question before you pay</span>
        <p>
          The Consolidated Fee Schedule carries a footnote about a fee
          that covers the cost of a future basement permit and its
          inspections, so that a homeowner who later finishes the basement
          gets the permit and inspections at no charge. The current
          schedule does not show which fee that footnote belongs to or
          which homes it covers. Ask the Building Department whether it
          applies to yours.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="inspections">Inspections</h2>
      <p>
        Saratoga Springs does not currently publish a basement-specific
        inspection list on its website. The order comes from the
        residential code
        (<a href={src.ircCh1} rel="noopener">IRC R109</a>, as amended by
        <a href={src.utahAmendments} rel="noopener">Utah Code
        15A-3-202</a>): rough plumbing, mechanical, gas, and electrical
        before anything is covered, then framing once the rough-ins have
        passed, then insulation, which Utah's amendments let the building
        official inspect, and a final inspection when the work is complete
        and before the space is used. Work can't continue past a stage
        until its inspection is approved.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Requesting one.</strong> The city lists an inspection
          request line:
          <a
            href={cityPhone.href}
            data-event="city_contact_click"
            data-event-label="saratoga-inspection-phone"
          >{cityPhone.display}</a>, extension 120.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Days.</strong> The Building Department is open Monday
          through Friday, with a limited number of inspections on Fridays.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>On site.</strong> All permit documents must be on site
          for inspections. The Building Department's page asks for the
          approved plans printed at 18 by 24 inches or larger.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Reinspections.</strong> Billed at $47 per hour, with a
          one hour minimum.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Expiration.</strong> Under the code, a permit lapses if
          work does not start within 180 days, or if more than 180 days
          pass between inspections, unless the building official extends
          it in writing
          (<a href={src.ircCh1} rel="noopener">IRC R105.5</a>).
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="iadu">Basement apartments (IADUs)</h2>
      <p>
        Saratoga Springs calls a basement apartment an internal accessory
        dwelling unit, or IADU. Whether you can have one depends first on
        where you live. The rules are in
        <a href={src.aduCode} rel="noopener">Chapter 19.20 of the city's
        Land Development Code</a>
        and the
        <a href={src.aduRegistration} rel="noopener">ADU registration
        packet</a>.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Check the map first.</strong> The city sorts
          neighborhoods into IADU Allowed, IADU Prohibited, and IADU
          Potential, and posts an interactive map on its
          <a href={src.iaduFaq} rel="noopener">accessory unit FAQ
          page</a>. The registration packet says most plats recorded
          before October 1, 2021 are not eligible. An HOA can still
          prohibit one where the city allows it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Lot size.</strong> Greater than 6,000 square feet.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Owner occupied.</strong> The owner must live in the
          home, in either unit. The code allows a temporary absence of
          three years or less for a job assignment, sabbatical, military
          service, or voluntary service.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What the unit needs.</strong> Its own sleeping area,
          kitchen, and bathroom, and a separate exterior entrance.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Parking.</strong> One paved off-street stall for the
          IADU, at least 9 by 18 feet, on top of the home's required
          parking. Street parking does not count.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Registration.</strong> Register the unit with the city
          (a $50 review fee) in addition to the building permit, and get
          an occupancy permit before anyone moves in. The packet says most
          applications get a response within 10 to 12 business days after
          they are routed to staff.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No short-term rentals.</strong> The city does not allow
          rentals of less than 30 days.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A second kitchen is not an IADU.</strong> The city's FAQ
          says adding one does not let you split the house into two units
          without the permit and the other IADU requirements.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If registration is not completed, the code says the unit is not
        considered legal or approved, and it allows fines and a lien on
        the property.
      </p>

      <div class="glance glance--end">
        <span class="eyebrow">What Saratoga Springs does not publish</span>
        <ul>
          <li>A basement-specific inspection list.</li>
          <li>
            A flat fee, or the per-square-foot value it uses for a
            basement finish.
          </li>
          <li>Which plan review percentage applies to a basement finish.</li>
          <li>A review time for basement permits.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          For those, the Building Department is the source. Building in
          Lehi instead? See the
          <a href="/lehi-basement-finishing-permit/">Lehi permit
          guide</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <span class="eyebrow">Quick Answers</span>
      <h2>Saratoga Springs basement permit FAQs</h2>
      <FAQAccordion faqs={permitFaqs} />
      <p class="more-links">
        More about the work itself:
        <a href="/saratoga-springs-basement-finishing/">basement finishing
        in Saratoga Springs</a>,
        <a href="/process/">our six-step process</a>, and
        <a href="/faq/">general FAQs</a>. For the statewide rule, see
        <a href="/utah-basement-finishing-permit/">do you need a permit to
        finish a basement in Utah?</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <h2 id="sources">Sources</h2>
      <p>
        Checked on
        <time datetime={lastChecked.iso}>{lastChecked.display}</time>.
        Cities replace documents, so if a PDF link breaks, the city page
        named beside it has the current copy.
      </p>
      <ol class="sources">
        <li>
          City of Saratoga Springs,
          <a href={src.basementPermit} rel="noopener">Obtaining a Basement
          Permit</a>: plan requirements, the portal, the code checklist,
          the inspection request line.
        </li>
        <li>
          City of Saratoga Springs,
          <a href={src.building} rel="noopener">Building Department</a>:
          responsibilities, hours, paperless submittals, codes in use.
        </li>
        <li>
          City of Saratoga Springs,
          <a href={src.buildingPermit} rel="noopener">Obtaining a Building
          Permit</a>: the deposit, plan changes, the processing time for
          single-family homes.
        </li>
        <li>
          City of Saratoga Springs,
          <a href={src.forms} rel="noopener">Applications and Forms</a>:
          the Owner Builder Certificate.
        </li>
        <li>
          City of Saratoga Springs,
          <a href={src.feeSchedule} rel="noopener">Consolidated Fee
          Schedule</a>
          (PDF), Building section, and the
          <a href={src.permitFeeTable} rel="noopener">Building Permit Fee
          Schedule</a>
          (PDF, linked from the Building Department page).
        </li>
        <li>
          City of Saratoga Springs,
          <a href={src.aduCode} rel="noopener">Land Development Code
          Chapter 19.20, Accessory Dwelling Units</a>
          (PDF), the
          <a href={src.aduRegistration} rel="noopener">ADU registration
          packet</a>
          (PDF), and the
          <a href={src.iaduFaq} rel="noopener">FAQs for Accessory Units
          and Renting</a>
          page with the IADU map.
        </li>
